SAS has a built-in procedure called `ods` (Output Delivery System) that allows you to generate output files in various formats, including PDF. To output a PDF file in a journal format, you can use the `ods pdf` statement and specify the journal format using the ` Journalize` option. Here's an example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" startpage=never;
ods graphics / width=6.5in height=8.5in;
/* your SAS code here */;
ods pdf close;
```
The `ods pdf` statement tells SAS to output the results to a PDF file named "output.pdf". The `startpage=never` option tells SAS not to include a title page at the beginning of the PDF file. The `ods graphics` statement sets the width and height of the graphics area.

To get the journal format, you'll need to use the `Journalize` option. Here's an updated example:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" startpage=never journalize;
ods graphics / width=6.5in height=8.5in;
/* your SAS code here */;
ods pdf close;
```
The `Journalize` option tells SAS to format the output in a journal style, with a header, footer, and margins.

You can customize the journal format further by using additional options, such as:

* `header`: specifies the title and footer text
* `footer`: specifies the footer text
* `margin`: specifies the margin size
* `orientation`: specifies the page orientation (e.g., portrait or landscape)

Here's an updated example with customizations:
```sas
ods pdf file="output.pdf" startpage=never journalize header="My Journal Title" footer="Page %P of %N" margin=0.5in orientation=portrait;
ods graphics / width=6.5in height=8.5in;
/* your SAS code here */;
ods pdf close;
```
