You want to add a link to every post on your website, allowing users to visit the post page. This is often referred to as a "permalink" or "read more" link.

To achieve this, you can modify your Nginx configuration to include the post ID or slug in the URL, and then use this information to serve the corresponding post page.

Assuming you have a database of posts with IDs and slugs, and you use a template engine like Twig or Jinja2, you can add a custom variable to your templates to generate the permalink.

Here's an example of how you can modify your Nginx configuration to include the post ID in the URL:
```nginx
location / {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    index index.html index.htm;

    try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;

    # Add post ID to the URL
    if ($uri ~ "(.*)/(.*)") {
        set $post_id $2;
    }

    # Serve the post page
    if ($post_id != "") {
        rewrite ^(.*)$ /post/$post_id permanent;
    }
}
```
In the above code, we're using a regular expression to capture the post ID from the URL path. We then use the `set` directive to store the captured value in the `$post_id` variable.

Next, we use an `if` statement to check if the `$post_id` variable is set. If it is, we redirect the request to the corresponding post page.

You'll need to modify your `post` directory to serve the post pages. For example:
```nginx
location /post/ {
    root /usr/share/nginx/html/post;

    try_files $uri $uri/index.html;
}
```
In this example, we're telling Nginx to look for a file named `index.html` in the `/post/` directory (or a subdirectory) and serve it if it exists.

To generate the permalink in your templates, you can use a custom variable. For example, in Twig:
```twig
{# assuming $post has the post data #}
<a href="{{ '_site/path/to/post/' ~ post.id }}">Read more</a>
```
In Jinja2:
```jinja2
<a href="{{ path('_site/path/to/post/') ~ post.id }}">Read more</a>
```
In both cases, we're concatenating the `_site/path/to/post/` string with the `post.id` variable to generate the permalink.

